## Live session tracking

Sessions live in a module-level `Map<string, PtyRecord>` in `src/main/services/ptyManager.ts:38`:

```ts
const ptys = new Map<string, PtyRecord>();
```

Each `PtyRecord` (defined at `src/main/services/ptyManager.ts:21-36`) holds the `node-pty` process (`proc`), `cwd`, `owner` (the `WebContents` that gets `pty:data:`/`pty:exit:` events), a `kind` (`'agent' | 'shell' | 'tui' | 'service'`), `taskId`/`featureId`, and a headless `TerminalMirror` that buffers all output for reattach/persistence. PTYs are registered on spawn (`ptys.set(...)` at lines 522, 645, 906) and removed on exit or teardown.

## Resize

`resizePty` at `src/main/services/ptyManager.ts:695-705`:

```ts
export function resizePty(id: string, cols: number, rows: number): void {
  const record = ptys.get(id);
  if (record) {
    record.mirror?.resize(cols, rows);
    try {
      record.proc.resize(cols, rows);
    } catch {
      // EBADF can happen during transitions
    }
  }
}
```

It resizes both the headless mirror (so serialized/reattach state matches) and the real PTY, wrapped in a try/catch since `EBADF` can occur mid-transition (e.g., process exiting).

## Shutdown and timeout

`gracefulKillProc` at `src/main/services/ptyManager.ts:727-763` sends `SIGTERM` first, waits for the process's `onExit`, and escalates to `SIGKILL` only if a timeout fires first:

```ts
const GRACEFUL_KILL_TIMEOUT_MS = 3000;  // ptyManager.ts:708
```

It races `proc.onExit()` against a 3-second `setTimeout`; whichever resolves first calls `finish()` (clearing the other). This is deliberate — plain `proc.kill()` in node-pty sends `SIGHUP`, which Claude Code doesn't trap, so the in-memory session tail was lost on refresh/quit (comment at `ptyManager.ts:715-718`). SIGTERM gives Claude time to flush its session jsonl before the hard kill.

This is used by `killPtyInternal` (line 777), `killAll` (line 808, awaits all in parallel — bounded by ~one grace window via `Promise.all`), and `killByOwner` (line 825, fire-and-forget on window close).

## Environment specialness

Built by `buildDirectEnv` at `src/main/services/ptyManager.ts:231-340` (used for direct/agent spawns; the shell-fallback path in `startPty` at lines 590-615 does something similar but simpler):

- **Minimal by default**: unless `syncShellEnv` is on, the base env is empty (`{}` at line 233-235) rather than inheriting `process.env` — for fast, predictable spawns.
- `TERM_PROGRAM: 'dash'`, `HOME`, merged `PATH` (with Dash-managed `rtk` bin dir prepended, line 239-242), and `COLORFGBG` set from the app's dark/light theme so CLI apps like `ls`/`fzf` pick contrasting colors (line 251).
- Platform branch: Windows gets a curated set of system vars (`APPDATA`, `SystemRoot`, `COMSPEC`, etc., lines 259-276); Unix gets `TERM=xterm-256color`, `COLORTERM=truecolor`, `USER` (lines 279-281).
- When not syncing the full shell env, an explicit auth passthrough allowlist (`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`, `GH_TOKEN`, proxy vars) is copied in (lines 286-303).
- User-configured env vars are merged, but filtered against `RESERVED_ENV_KEYS` (`PATH`, `HOME`, `USER`, `TERM`, `COLORTERM`, `TERM_PROGRAM`, `COLORFGBG`, `DASH_HOOK_PORT` — lines 127-138) so a user override can't break spawn internals.
- Per-task port env vars are injected from `WorkspacePortsRuntime.getEnvForWorktree(cwd)` (line 321) so commands Claude runs resolve the same ports shown in the UI's ports panel.
- `CLAUDE_CODE_NO_FLICKER=1` (line 327) disables Claude Code's built-in viewport scrolling since Dash renders its own terminal viewport.
- `DASH_HOOK_PORT` (line 336) is set last (winning over any inherited value) to point Claude Code's hooks at the live `HookServer`; hook scripts guard on this being unset so sessions launched outside Dash no-op instead of erroring.
